ING-HUB-PROJECT

APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
- The application allows users to register and log into the system; they have a shopping cart where they can add products.

APIs

- addProduct - adds a product to the cart
- deleteProduct - deletes a product from the cart
- findCart - finds the cart for the user that is logged in
- findProductInCart - finds a product in the cart for the user that is logged in
- changePrice - changes the price of a product in the cart

SECURITY
- To use user registration and login, the application used Swagger to provide a graphical interface.
- The application used a simple security implementation based on JWT tokens.
- When the user logs in, the user receive a token that can be used in the header for authorization.
- The login and register APIs can be used by anyone.
- Every HTTP call is intercepted and checked to verify whether the user is allowed to use it (if the user are logged in, if the user have permissions, etc.).
- The password is hashed using the BCrypt algorithm, the hash is stored in the Postgres database, and the login verifies it.
- The application has two roles: Admin and User. Any newly created user will be assigned the USER role.
- Initializer: The admin account is created at startup if it does not exist. The environment variables DEFAULT_ADMIN_USER and DEFAULT_ADMIN_PASS must be set for the account creation.
- Filter: With this filter, Spring validates any endpoint that requires special conditions, such as the user being logged in or the user having a specific role.
    - read the token from header
    - extract username from token
    - get user from database
    - validate token
    - set user in security context

JPA
- The application uses JPA to interact with the Postgres database.
  RELATIONS:
    USER -> ONE TO ONE -> CART
    CART -> ONE TO MANY -> PRODUCT

Exception Handling
- The application uses a exception handler(class ExceptionHandlerController) to catch exceptions and return appropriate HTTP responses.

Mapper
- The application uses MapStruct to map between DTOs and entities.

KAFKA
- The application uses Kafka to save data for auditing.
Example: when a user is created, the application sends a message to the Kafka topic "AUDIT-IN" with the audit data.
The application has a Kafka consumer that listens to the "AUDIT-IN" topic and saves the audit data to the Postgres database in the "Audit" table.

AUDIT
- The application has an audit table that records absolutely everything that happens, and there is an endpoint that returns all audit data from the database, but only users with the ADMIN role can access this endpoint.

DOCKER
- In the application package, there is a docker-compose.yaml file that contains the containers necessary for the application — Postgres and Kafka.

